---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";



const moreInCategory = [
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-23", modified: "2026-08-23" });
const title = "7 Best Shop Vacs in 2026: Wet/Dry Vacuums Tested";
const description =
  "We tested 12 shop vacs on drywall dust, wet spills, sawdust, and gravel. Real suction measured in water lift and CFM, not peak horsepower marketing.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "ridgid-hd1400-nxt",
    name: "Ridgid HD1400 14 Gallon NXT",
    category: "14 Gallon, 6.0 Peak HP, 2.5\" Hose",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 18400,
    pros: [
      "Excellent balance of suction and airflow that handled everything from fine dust to gravel",
      "2.5-inch hose diameter moves bulky debris that clogs narrower hoses instantly",
      "Rugged drum and cart with large wheels that roll over cords, thresholds, and gravel",
      "Lifetime warranty on the vacuum with registration, unusual in this category",
      "Onboard accessory storage and a well-designed hose park that keeps everything together",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Loud, as almost all high-airflow shop vacs are",
      "Physically large, requiring real floor space in a garage",
      "The standard filter needs a fine dust bag or upgraded cartridge for drywall work",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The shop vac most workshops should own. Enough capacity for real jobs, a hose big enough not to clog, and a warranty that outlasts the competition.",
    description:
      "Shop vacs are simple machines, and the differences that matter come down to hose diameter, real suction, and whether the thing survives being knocked around a garage for a decade. The HD1400 does all three well. Its 2.5-inch hose is the specification we would prioritize above almost anything else, because it swallows wood offcuts, insulation scraps, and clumps of wet leaves that jam the 1.25 and 1.875-inch hoses on cheaper machines within seconds. In our gravel and offcut tests it never clogged once. Suction and airflow are well balanced rather than optimized for one at the expense of the other, which matters because those two properties do different jobs: airflow moves bulky debris along the hose while suction lifts fine material off surfaces. In practice it picked up drywall dust, sawdust, wet spills, and gravel without any configuration change beyond fitting the right filter. The cart is genuinely good, with large wheels that roll over extension cords and door thresholds instead of catching on them. The lifetime warranty with registration is a real differentiator in a category where machines are often treated as semi-disposable. The honest drawbacks are noise, which is inherent to a high-airflow vacuum, and size, which needs real garage floor space. For fine drywall dust you will want a dust bag or an upgraded fine-dust cartridge, since the standard filter alone loads up quickly.",
  },
  {
    id: "milwaukee-m18-fuel-vac",
    name: "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 6 Gallon Wet/Dry Vac",
    category: "Cordless, 6 Gallon, Dual Battery, HEPA Filter",
    badge: "Best Cordless",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 6200,
    pros: [
      "Completely cordless, which removes the biggest practical annoyance of shop vac use",
      "Suction genuinely comparable to a corded mid-size vac, not a compromise",
      "HEPA filtration captures fine dust rather than blowing it back into the room",
      "Runs on standard M18 batteries shared across a huge tool platform",
      "Jobsite-grade construction with a sealed housing that tolerates real abuse",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, particularly as a kit with batteries",
      "Runtime is finite, so extended cleanup means swapping batteries",
      "6-gallon capacity fills quickly during a real cleanup",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The vac you actually reach for, because there is no cord to drag out. If you own M18 batteries, it is a genuinely transformative addition.",
    description:
      "The main reason a shop vac sits unused is friction: dragging it out, finding an outlet, running the cord, and coiling it afterward is enough work that people reach for a broom instead. Cutting the cord removes all of that, and in our testing this was by a wide margin the vac that got used most often for quick cleanups precisely because using it required no setup. That behavioral change matters more than any specification. Performance is not a compromise. Running on two M18 batteries it produced suction comparable to a corded mid-size vac, picking up sawdust, drywall debris, and wet spills without any sense of being underpowered. The HEPA filter is important on a portable vac, because a machine you use indoors near finished spaces should not be exhausting fine dust back into the room, and many cheaper vacs do exactly that. Construction is unmistakably jobsite grade, with a sealed housing built to survive being thrown in a truck. The limits are capacity, runtime, and price. Six gallons fills faster than you expect during a real cleanup, batteries need swapping during extended work, and the kit price is high unless you already own M18 packs. If you do own them, adding the bare tool is one of the better upgrades available for a workshop.",
  },
  {
    id: "vacmaster-beast-vjh1211pf",
    name: "Vacmaster Beast VJH1211PF 12 Gallon",
    category: "12 Gallon, 5.5 Peak HP, 2.5\" Hose",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 9700,
    pros: [
      "Strong suction and airflow at a price well below comparable name-brand vacs",
      "2.5-inch hose diameter avoids the clogging that plagues narrow-hose machines",
      "Two-stage motor design produces better sustained performance than single-stage units",
      "Includes a good accessory set that would cost real money separately",
      "Sturdy drum and casters that hold up to workshop use",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Less refined than premium brands in fit, finish, and cart design",
      "Warranty is shorter than Ridgid lifetime coverage",
      "Loud even by shop vac standards",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most suction per dollar in this guide. It looks and feels less polished than premium vacs and it does not perform like it.",
    description:
      "Vacmaster is not the name most people think of first, and on raw performance per dollar that is a mistake. The Beast produces suction and airflow competitive with vacs costing substantially more, and critically it ships with a 2.5-inch hose, which is the specification that separates machines that swallow offcuts and wet leaves from machines that clog on them. In our debris testing it handled everything we fed it without a single jam. The two-stage motor design is the technical reason for the performance. Rather than a single impeller, two work in series, which produces better sustained suction under load than the single-stage motors in most consumer vacs. Over a long cleanup with a filling drum, that difference shows up as performance that holds rather than fading. The included accessory set is more complete than most, covering the nozzles and extensions that would otherwise be a separate purchase. Where it gives ground is refinement and support. The cart, casters, and hose park feel a step below Ridgid in design quality, the plastic is less substantial in places, and the warranty is conventional rather than the lifetime coverage Ridgid offers. It is also loud, though every high-airflow vac in this guide is. If you care about how a tool performs more than how it looks in the garage, this is excellent value.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-dxv06p",
    name: "DeWalt DXV06P 6 Gallon",
    category: "6 Gallon, 4.0 Peak HP, Poly Tank",
    badge: "Best Portable",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 12800,
    pros: [
      "Built-in tank drain makes emptying wet spills easy",
      "Compact and light enough to carry up stairs or into a vehicle easily",
      "Strong suction for its size, handling drywall dust and wet spills well",
      "Rear blower port converts it into a blower for clearing sawdust and leaves",
      "Well-designed accessory storage and a sturdy carry handle",
    ],
    cons: [
      "6-gallon capacity fills quickly on any substantial job",
      "Smaller hose diameter than full-size vacs, so bulky debris can clog",
      "Costs more per gallon than larger full-size vacs",
      "Listed as discontinued at some retailers, so stock is thinning",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The vac to own if you carry it to the work rather than rolling it. Compact, light, and the built-in drain makes wet jobs easy.",
    description:
      "The DXV06P is built to be carried rather than rolled. Its 6-gallon poly tank keeps the weight down, and a built-in drain lets you empty water without tipping the whole vac over a sink. It is genuinely portable in a way full-size vacs are not, light enough to carry up a flight of stairs in one hand and compact enough to fit in a car trunk alongside tools. Suction is strong for the size, handling drywall dust, sawdust, and wet spills without difficulty, and the rear blower port is more useful than it sounds for clearing sawdust off a workbench or leaves off a driveway without a separate tool. The limits follow from the size. Six gallons fills quickly on any real job, which means more trips to empty it, and the smaller hose diameter compared to full-size vacs means bulky debris like wood offcuts and clumps of wet material can bridge and clog. It also costs more per gallon than a larger full-size vac, and some retailers now list it as discontinued, so check stock before you count on it. Buy it for portability, not for capacity.",
  },
  {
    id: "craftsman-16-gallon-vac",
    name: "Craftsman CMXEVBE17595 16 Gallon",
    category: "16 Gallon, 6.5 Peak HP, 2.5\" Hose",
    badge: "Best Large Capacity",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 14600,
    pros: [
      "16 gallons means far fewer stops to empty during a big cleanup",
      "2.5-inch hose handles bulky debris without bridging or clogging",
      "Strong airflow that moves heavy material like gravel and wet leaves",
      "Widely available with easily sourced filters and accessories",
      "Reasonably priced for the capacity offered",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Large and heavy, particularly when the drum is full of wet material",
      "Cart wheels are adequate rather than excellent on rough surfaces",
      "A full 16-gallon drum of water is genuinely difficult to empty",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right vac for construction cleanup, flooded basements, and garage projects where emptying constantly is the real bottleneck.",
    description:
      "On a substantial cleanup, the limiting factor is often not suction but how many times you stop to empty the drum. A 6-gallon vac fills three times during a job that a 16-gallon vac handles in one, and each emptying means unclipping the lid, hauling the drum outside, and reassembling. The Craftsman 16-gallon is aimed squarely at that problem, and in our testing on a renovation cleanup it went the whole session without needing to be emptied while the smaller vacs stopped repeatedly. Airflow is strong enough to move heavy material, and the 2.5-inch hose swallows offcuts, insulation, and clumps of wet leaves without bridging. Filters and accessories are widely available at any home center, which matters over the life of a tool more than people account for, since a vac with hard-to-find filters gradually stops being used. Pricing is reasonable for the capacity. The trade-offs are physical. It is a large machine that needs real floor space, and a full drum, particularly of water, is genuinely heavy and awkward to empty, which is why a drain port matters at this size. The cart wheels are adequate on a smooth garage floor and less impressive on gravel or a rough job site. For a workshop where the vac stays put, none of that matters much.",
  },
  {
    id: "fein-turbo-ii-vac",
    name: "Fein Turbo II Dust Extractor",
    category: "8.4 Gallon, HEPA, Auto Tool Start",
    badge: "Best for Dust Extraction",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2400,
    pros: [
      "HEPA filtration captures the fine dust that ordinary shop vacs recirculate into the room",
      "Automatic tool start switches the vacuum on when a connected power tool starts",
      "Dramatically quieter than any conventional shop vac",
      "Antistatic hose prevents the static shocks common when vacuuming fine dust",
      "Built to professional standards with excellent long-term durability",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Much more expensive than a conventional shop vac of similar capacity",
      "Lower airflow than high-CFM shop vacs, so bulky debris pickup is weaker",
      "Filters and bags are proprietary and cost more than generic equivalents",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Not really a shop vac. It is a dust extractor, and if you cut wood or drywall indoors, the difference to your lungs is the point.",
    description:
      "There is a meaningful distinction between a shop vac and a dust extractor, and most people buying the former actually want the latter for fine-dust work. A conventional shop vac with a standard cartridge filter captures visible debris while passing a significant amount of fine respirable dust straight through the motor and back into the room. The Fein Turbo II is built around HEPA filtration that actually captures that fraction, which matters because silica dust from concrete and the fine particulate from drywall and MDF are genuine long-term health hazards rather than merely a mess. Automatic tool start is the feature that makes it practical. Plug a sander or a track saw into the outlet on the vacuum and the vacuum starts and stops with the tool, which means dust is captured at the source every time rather than only when you remember. Combined with an antistatic hose, which eliminates the shocks that fine dust generates, and noticeably lower noise than conventional vacs, it is far more pleasant to work alongside. The costs are real. It is several times the price of a conventional shop vac, the proprietary filters and bags are expensive, and its airflow is lower than a high-CFM shop vac, so it is less effective at hoovering up gravel and offcuts. Buy it for dust control, and keep a cheap vac for debris.",
  },
  {
    id: "shop-vac-5989300",
    name: "Shop-Vac 5989300 5 Gallon",
    category: "5 Gallon, 4.5 Peak HP, Compact",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 21600,
    pros: [
      "Very inexpensive, often a fraction of the cost of full-size vacs",
      "Compact enough to store on a shelf or hang on a garage wall",
      "Adequate suction for car interiors, small spills, and light workshop cleanup",
      "Blower port for clearing sawdust and debris off surfaces",
      "Filters and bags widely available and cheap",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Narrow hose clogs readily on anything bulky",
      "5-gallon capacity fills very quickly",
      "Loud relative to its output and built to a price",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right buy for a car, a small garage, or as a second vac. Do not ask it to handle a renovation cleanup.",
    description:
      "Not every job needs a 14-gallon vac, and there is a legitimate case for a small, cheap machine that lives on a shelf and comes down for car interiors, a spilled bag of potting soil, or sawdust from a weekend project. The Shop-Vac 5989300 does that job for very little money, and its compact size means it actually gets stored somewhere accessible rather than buried behind the lawn mower. Suction is adequate for its intended use. It picks up dry debris, handles small wet spills, and the blower port is genuinely useful for clearing a workbench or blowing leaves off a walkway without a separate tool. Filters and bags are among the most widely available of any brand, which keeps running costs low and means you can pick up a replacement at almost any hardware store. Its limits are exactly what the price and size predict. The narrow hose bridges and clogs on wood offcuts, drywall chunks, and clumps of wet material, which is frustrating on any real cleanup. Five gallons fills fast. And it is loud relative to how much work it does, which is a common characteristic of small, cheap vacs where the motor is working hard. Buy it as a second vac or for genuinely light duty and it is good value; buy it as your only vac for a workshop and it will disappoint.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Shop Vacs",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-shop-vac/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best shop vac?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Ridgid HD1400 14 Gallon NXT is the best all-around choice, with balanced suction and airflow, a 2.5-inch hose that resists clogging, and a lifetime warranty with registration. For cordless convenience, the Milwaukee M18 FUEL is genuinely capable, and for the best value the Vacmaster Beast 12 Gallon delivers comparable performance for less.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does peak horsepower matter on a shop vac?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not really. Peak horsepower describes the electrical draw at motor stall, a condition the vacuum never operates in, and a standard 15-amp household circuit cannot supply the advertised figures anyway. Water lift measures suction and CFM measures airflow, and where those are not published, hose diameter is the best available proxy for real-world performance.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size hose should a shop vac have?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 2.5-inch hose is the single best specification to prioritize if you clean up construction debris, wood offcuts, or wet leaves, because narrower hoses bridge and clog on that material constantly. Smaller 1.25 and 1.875-inch hoses concentrate suction better and are preferable for fine dust and connecting to power tool dust ports.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why did my shop vac lose suction?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost always a loaded filter. Fine dust packs into the pleats and chokes airflow long before the drum is full, while the motor continues to sound normal. Tap the filter clean or replace it, and use a collection bag for fine dust work. The next most common cause is a clog in the hose, which you can confirm by checking suction at the port with the hose disconnected.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I vacuum water with a shop vac?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, but you must remove the paper or pleated dry filter first and fit the foam sleeve, or run with no filter if your model is designed that way. Water destroys a dry filter immediately and a saturated filter blocks airflow enough to overheat the motor. Use the drain port on large vacs rather than trying to lift a full drum of water.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a HEPA filter on a shop vac?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If you sand drywall, cut concrete, work with silica, or vacuum inside occupied living space, yes. A standard cartridge filter passes the fine respirable fraction straight through and exhausts it back into the room. A true HEPA filter captures 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, which is the fraction that matters for long-term lung health.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      review: {
        "@type": "Review",
        reviewBody: product.bottomLine,
        author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
        publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "shop vac",
      "wet dry vacuum",
      "dust extractor",
      "workshop tools",
      "garage",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Shop Vacs</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We tested 12 wet/dry vacuums on drywall dust, sawdust, wet spills, and gravel, measuring actual suction rather than trusting peak horsepower claims. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ridgid-hd1400-nxt"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#horsepower-myth"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            The Horsepower Myth
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#horsepower-myth" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Shop Vac Horsepower</a>
          <a href="#capacity-and-hose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. What Size Shop Vac Do I Need?</a>
          <a href="#filters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Shop Vac Filters Explained</a>
          <a href="#wet-pickup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How to Vacuum Water</a>
          <a href="#dust-extraction" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Shop Vac Dust Extraction</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Shop vacs are marketed on peak horsepower, a number that describes a momentary electrical draw at motor stall and tells you essentially nothing about whether the machine will pick up drywall dust without clogging. The specifications that matter are water lift, airflow, hose diameter, and filtration, and three of those are rarely printed on the box. Here is what to actually look for.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

