        <!-- SECTION 2: The Peak Horsepower Myth -->
        <div id="horsepower-myth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Shop Vac Horsepower: Why Peak HP Misleads
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every shop vac box leads with a peak horsepower figure, and it is close to meaningless as a measure of cleaning ability. Understanding why redirects your attention to the numbers that matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Peak horsepower measures a momentary stall condition.</strong> It is derived from the electrical draw at the instant the motor is prevented from turning, a state the vacuum never operates in. A machine advertising 6.0 peak horsepower on a standard household circuit is not producing anywhere near that in use, because a 15-amp circuit physically cannot supply that much sustained power.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water lift measures suction.</strong> Expressed in inches of water lift, this is how much vertical column of water the vacuum can pull, and it corresponds to the machine ability to lift heavy or embedded material off a surface. Higher water lift picks up wet material, gravel, and debris compacted into a corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>CFM measures airflow.</strong> Cubic feet per minute is the volume of air the vacuum moves, and it determines how well material travels along the hose once picked up. High airflow carries bulky debris to the drum; low airflow leaves it sitting in the hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The two trade against each other.</strong> A narrow hose raises water lift and lowers airflow; a wide hose does the opposite. This is why a dust extractor with a small hose has excellent suction and clogs on wood chips, while a shop vac with a 2.5-inch hose swallows offcuts but is less aggressive at pulling fine dust out of a crevice.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>What to actually compare.</strong> Where manufacturers publish water lift and CFM, use those. Where they do not, hose diameter is the single best available proxy for real-world usefulness on debris, and filtration type is the best proxy for usefulness on fine dust.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
