        <!-- SECTION 3: Capacity, Hose Diameter, and What Actually Limits You -->
        <div id="capacity-and-hose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Size Shop Vac Do I Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two physical specifications do more to determine your experience than the motor does, and one of them is almost never mentioned in reviews.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shop Vac Hose Diameter Is the Most Important Spec</h3>

          <p>
            Shop vac hoses come in roughly 1.25, 1.875, and 2.5-inch diameters. The difference is not incremental. A 2.5-inch hose swallows wood offcuts, drywall chunks, insulation scraps, and clumps of wet leaves that bridge and jam instantly in a 1.25-inch hose. If you clean up after construction or yard work, this specification matters more than motor power, capacity, or brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smaller Hoses Have Their Place</h3>

          <p>
            A narrower hose concentrates suction, which makes it better for fine dust, car interiors, crevices, and connecting to power tool dust ports. Many vacs ship with adapters so you can step down when needed. The mistake is buying a machine whose largest available hose is narrow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shop Vac Capacity Determines How Often You Stop</h3>

          <p>
            Drum capacity does not affect performance at all, only how often you empty it. Five to six gallons suits car cleanups and light workshop use. Twelve to sixteen gallons suits renovation cleanup, garage work, and wet pickup. The practical ceiling is that a full drum of water becomes genuinely difficult to lift, which is why larger vacs should have a drain port.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hose Length and Cord Length Matter Too</h3>

          <p>
            A vac with a short hose and short cord has to be repositioned constantly, which is the friction that keeps machines sitting unused. Longer hoses lose some airflow, but the convenience usually outweighs it for household use. Check both figures, since they are often modest on budget machines.
          </p>

