        <!-- SECTION 4: Filters: The Part Everyone Gets Wrong -->
        <div id="filters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Shop Vac Filters Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filter choice determines whether a shop vac cleans the air or redistributes fine dust into it, and using the wrong one is both the most common mistake and the one with real health consequences.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standard Cartridge Filters</h3>

          <p>
            The pleated filter that comes with most vacs handles general debris and coarse dust well. It does not capture the fine respirable fraction, which passes through and exits with the exhaust. For sweeping a garage this is fine; for drywall sanding it means you are aerosolizing dust rather than removing it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fine Dust Filters and Bags</h3>

          <p>
            A fine dust cartridge combined with a collection bag is the practical answer for drywall and sanding work on a conventional vac. The bag captures the bulk before it reaches the filter, which both improves capture and prevents the filter clogging within minutes. Skipping the bag on fine dust work is why people conclude their vac lost suction.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">HEPA Filters</h3>

          <p>
            A true HEPA filter captures 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, which includes the respirable dust that matters most. For anyone cutting concrete, working with silica, sanding drywall regularly, or vacuuming in occupied living space, this is the filtration level worth having. It costs more and requires the vacuum body to be well sealed to be meaningful.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foam Sleeves for Wet Pickup</h3>

          <p>
            Wet vacuuming requires removing the paper or pleated filter entirely and fitting a foam sleeve, or using no filter at all depending on the model. Vacuuming water through a paper filter destroys it immediately and can damage the motor. This is the single most common way shop vac filters get ruined.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Why Your Shop Vac Lost Suction</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Nine times out of ten it is a loaded filter, not a failing motor. Fine dust packs into the pleats and chokes airflow long before the drum is full, and the machine sounds like it is running fine while picking up almost nothing. Tap the filter clean or replace it, and use a collection bag for fine dust work. The other common cause is a clog in the hose, which you can find by disconnecting it and checking for suction at the port.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Wet Pickup Done Properly -->
        <div id="wet-pickup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Vacuum Water With a Shop Vac
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wet vacuuming is the capability that makes a shop vac genuinely valuable in a home, and it is also where most machines get damaged through simple procedural errors.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Remove the dry filter first, every time.</strong> Paper and pleated cartridge filters are destroyed by water immediately, and a saturated filter blocks airflow so completely that the motor can overheat. Most vacs include a foam sleeve for wet use, and some are designed to run with no filter at all for water. Check your manual once and remember the answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Empty the drum before it gets too heavy.</strong> Water weighs roughly eight pounds per gallon, so a full 14-gallon drum holds well over a hundred pounds. That is not liftable, which is why larger vacs include a drain port at the bottom. Use it rather than trying to tip the drum.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Know the float shutoff.</strong> Most wet/dry vacs have a float that rises with the water level and blocks the intake when the drum is full, protecting the motor. If your vac suddenly stops picking up during wet work and the motor note changes, that is the float doing its job rather than a fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never vacuum flammable liquids or hot ash.</strong> Gasoline, solvents, and similar liquids can be ignited by the motor, and hot embers vacuumed from a fireplace or grill will set the filter and drum contents alight. Ash vacuums with metal drums and specific filters exist for that job.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dry the vac out afterward.</strong> Leaving water sitting in the drum grows mold and corrodes metal components, and the smell that follows is difficult to remove. Drain it, rinse it, and leave the lid off to air dry before storage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Understand the limits on flooding.</strong> A shop vac is excellent for a burst pipe, an overflowing washing machine, or water in a basement corner. It is not a substitute for a pump on a genuinely flooded basement, where you would be emptying the drum every few minutes for hours.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Connecting to Power Tools for Dust Extraction -->
        <div id="dust-extraction" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Shop Vac Dust Extraction: Connecting to Power Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            A shop vac connected to a sander, saw, or router captures dust at the source, which is dramatically more effective than cleaning it up afterward and far better for your lungs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Adapters are the practical obstacle.</strong> Power tool dust ports come in a frustrating variety of diameters, and shop vac hoses come in three. A universal adapter set is inexpensive and solves most of it, and many vacs include a stepped adapter that fits several common port sizes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Smaller hoses work better for tool connection.</strong> A 1.25 or 1.875-inch hose concentrates suction at the tool port, which is what you want when pulling fine dust out of a sander shroud. The wide hose that excels at bulk debris is actually worse here, and it is also unwieldy hanging off a hand tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Automatic tool start is the feature that changes habits.</strong> Vacs with a switched outlet start when a connected tool starts and stop a few seconds after. This removes the step where you decide whether it is worth turning the vac on, which in practice means dust gets collected every time rather than sometimes. It is standard on dust extractors and available on some shop vacs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Antistatic hose prevents shocks.</strong> Fine dust moving through a plastic hose builds a substantial static charge, and the resulting shocks are unpleasant and can affect electronics. Antistatic hoses have a conductive path that dissipates it, and they are worth buying if you sand regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a separator for volume.</strong> A cyclone separator sits between the tool and the vac and drops the majority of debris into a bucket before it reaches the filter. On a machine used for woodworking, this extends filter life enormously and keeps suction consistent throughout a session.
          </p>
        </div>

