          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Ridgid HD1400 14 Gallon NXT is the best all-around choice, with balanced suction and airflow, a 2.5-inch hose that resists clogging, and a lifetime warranty with registration. For cordless convenience, the Milwaukee M18 FUEL is genuinely capable, and for the best value the Vacmaster Beast 12 Gallon delivers comparable performance for less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does peak horsepower matter on a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not really. Peak horsepower describes the electrical draw at motor stall, a condition the vacuum never operates in, and a standard 15-amp household circuit cannot supply the advertised figures anyway. Water lift measures suction and CFM measures airflow, and where those are not published, hose diameter is the best available proxy for real-world performance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size hose should a shop vac have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 2.5-inch hose is the single best specification to prioritize if you clean up construction debris, wood offcuts, or wet leaves, because narrower hoses bridge and clog on that material constantly. Smaller 1.25 and 1.875-inch hoses concentrate suction better and are preferable for fine dust and connecting to power tool dust ports.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my shop vac lose suction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always a loaded filter. Fine dust packs into the pleats and chokes airflow long before the drum is full, while the motor continues to sound normal. Tap the filter clean or replace it, and use a collection bag for fine dust work. The next most common cause is a clog in the hose, which you can confirm by checking suction at the port with the hose disconnected.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I vacuum water with a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but you must remove the paper or pleated dry filter first and fit the foam sleeve, or run with no filter if your model is designed that way. Water destroys a dry filter immediately and a saturated filter blocks airflow enough to overheat the motor. Use the drain port on large vacs rather than trying to lift a full drum of water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a HEPA filter on a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you sand drywall, cut concrete, work with silica, or vacuum inside occupied living space, yes. A standard cartridge filter passes the fine respirable fraction straight through and exhausts it back into the room. A true HEPA filter captures 99.97 percent of particles at 0.3 microns, which is the fraction that matters for long-term lung health.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
