        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Shop Vac Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shop vacs are simple, and the errors people make with them are simple too, but a few of them destroy filters or motors outright.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Water Without Removing the Dry Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A paper or pleated cartridge filter is destroyed by water immediately, and once saturated it blocks airflow so completely that the motor can overheat. Every wet/dry vac has a documented procedure for wet use, usually removing the cartridge and fitting a foam sleeve. Read it once and you will never destroy a filter again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Collection Bag on Fine Dust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drywall dust and sanding dust pack into a cartridge filter within minutes and choke suction while the machine still sounds like it is working. A collection bag captures the bulk before it reaches the filter, which keeps suction consistent and dramatically extends filter life. This is why professionals always bag fine dust work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Capacity Instead of Hose Diameter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 16-gallon vac with a 1.25-inch hose will clog on the same wood offcuts and wet leaves that an 8-gallon vac with a 2.5-inch hose swallows without hesitation. Capacity only determines how often you empty the drum; hose diameter determines whether the material gets there at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Hot Ash or Flammable Liquids</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Embers from a fireplace or grill can remain hot enough to ignite the filter and drum contents hours after they look dead, and gasoline or solvent vapors can be ignited by the motor. Dedicated ash vacuums with metal drums exist for the first case, and nothing in this guide should be used for the second.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

